<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manitoba Act</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1870">1870</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manitoba">Manitoba</a> Act, passed by the Parliament of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> in May 1870, created the province of Manitoba from part of Rupert's Land after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-river-resistance">Red River Resistance</a>. Negotiated with delegates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-riel">Louis Riel</a>'s provisional government, it provided for French and English as official languages, separate Catholic and Protestant schools and a land grant for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis</a> children. The new province was small, centred on the Red River settlement.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manitoba-act.jpg" alt="Manitoba Act" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Saffron Blaze · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Centre_Block_-_Parliament_Hill.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-river-colony">Red River Colony</a> lay in Rupert's Land, controlled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudsons-bay-company">Hudson's Bay Company</a>, which surrendered it to the British Crown under the Rupert's Land Act 1868; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> paid £300,000 for it, though Indigenous and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis people</a> lived there. The Métis were not consulted when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-mcdougall">William McDougall</a> was appointed lieutenant governor and set out for Red River in September 1869. Armed Métis barred his party at the 49th parallel, and on 16 December he gave up for the time being.</p><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-riel">Louis Riel</a>, chosen for his education and his command of French and English, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis</a> formed a provisional government that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottawa">Ottawa</a> considered illegal and occupied Fort Garry, where they held 45 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a> settlers. The execution of one prisoner, Thomas Scott, on 4 March 1870 outraged opinion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>. The provisional government drew up four successive lists of rights; the last, dated 9 May 1870, asked for entry as a province rather than a territory, a bilingual lieutenant governor and no legal consequences for its members.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The act, which received royal assent on 12 May 1870 and is part of the Constitution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manitoba">Manitoba</a> the fifth province and created its legislature and government. Laws had to be passed and published in French and English, either language could be used in the legislature and the courts, and denominational schools existing by law or practice at the union were protected, though teaching in French was not guaranteed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottawa">Ottawa</a> paid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a>, and the province received four seats in the House of Commons and two in the Senate. Section 31 set aside 1.4 million acres for the children of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis</a> families, beyond the land they already farmed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Colonel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garnet-wolseley">Garnet Wolseley</a>'s Red River Expedition followed, and militiamen seeking to avenge Scott carried out reprisals that newspapers called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reign-of-terror">Reign of Terror</a>; many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis</a> fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saskatchewan">Saskatchewan</a> and Riel to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. The land promised proved too little for the claims, and money scrip replaced it; historian D. N. Sprague notes that later laws took land from the Métis. In the 1890s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manitoba">Manitoba</a> ended funding for denominational schools, creating the Manitoba Schools Question, and made English its only official language, a law the Supreme Court found unconstitutional in 1985. Section 31 was central to the 2013 case Manitoba Métis Federation v. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>. The Constitution Act, 1871 confirmed Parliament's power to create provinces.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Manitoba Act, 1870” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Delegates sent to Ottawa negotiated the terms that became the <b>Manitoba Act</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-assiniboia">Provisional Government of Assiniboia</a></li></ul></section>
